{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_wCmheeBfVVLq5PBxwY6kGivF","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# human-as-mcp Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the Human-as-MCP interface for obtaining human input on-demand. This spec governs sync and async human call modes, call persistence, non-blocking execution, and the first-time bootstrap flow where project intent is captured directly from human input without pre-written requirement files.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Human Call Interface\n     7→The system SHALL define a standard human call interface supporting sync and async modes.\n     8→\n     9→**Sync mode** (human present):\n    10→- Ask directly via Claude Code conversation (AskUserQuestion)\n    11→- Use for: immediate decisions, requirement clarification, project direction\n    12→\n    13→**Async mode** (human unavailable):\n    14→- Write request file to `human-calls/`\n    15→- Use for: offline operations, cross-session pending requests\n    16→\n    17→**First-time bootstrap**: When entering an empty project, the agent SHALL ask the human \"What should this project do?\" via sync human call, then create an openspec change directly from the response. The change proposal captures the human's intent; specs formalize it. No intermediate demands file.\n    18→\n    19→#### Scenario: Sync call — first-time project intent\n    20→- **WHEN** agent enters an empty project\n    21→- **THEN** agent asks the human via sync call, creates an openspec change proposal from the response\n    22→\n    23→#### Scenario: Sync call — immediate decision\n    24→- **WHEN** agent needs a human decision and the human is present\n    25→- **THEN** agent asks directly via conversation\n    26→\n    27→#### Scenario: Async call — offline operation\n    28→- **WHEN** agent needs the human to perform an operation that requires offline time\n    29→- **THEN** agent writes a request to human-calls/, marks dependent tasks as waiting-human\n    30→\n    31→#### Scenario: Human responds to async call\n    32→- **WHEN** a human fills in the Response section of a pending request\n    33→- **THEN** next session processes the response and unblocks dependent work\n    34→\n    35→### Requirement: Non-Blocking Execution\n    36→Human calls MUST NOT block unrelated tasks.\n    37→\n    38→#### Scenario: Continue after issuing call\n    39→- **WHEN** agent issues a human call and other independent tasks exist\n    40→- **THEN** agent marks dependent tasks as waiting-human and continues other work\n    41→\n    42→### Requirement: Human Call Persistence\n    43→Async human call requests SHALL be persisted to the file system.\n    44→\n    45→Files stored in `human-calls/` with filename format `{YYYYMMDD}-{HHmmss}-{short-description}.md`.\n    46→\n    47→#### Scenario: Cross-session persistence\n    48→- **WHEN** session A's human call is unanswered at session end\n    49→- **THEN** the file persists and session B checks for responses on startup\n    50→\n    51→### Requirement: Human Call Types\n    52→The system SHALL support three types: decision, action, information.\n    53→\n    54→#### Scenario: Decision call\n    55→- **WHEN** agent needs the human to choose between options\n    56→- **THEN** the call lists all options with trade-off analysis\n    57→\n    58→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"a239fa7c-1472-40ae-8d46-d4fd158553f7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_8bVnO7Tf0uSMjMVc9UF4a1Cy","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# change-verifier Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Spec Scenario Extraction\n     7→The system SHALL extract all WHEN/THEN scenarios from a change's specs.\n     8→\n     9→#### Scenario: Extract scenarios from change\n    10→- **WHEN** `se3 verify --change <name>` is run\n    11→- **THEN** it parses all spec files in `openspec/changes/<name>/specs/`\n    12→- **AND** extracts a list of scenario IDs (format: `<spec-name>/<scenario-name>`)\n    13→\n    14→### Requirement: Implementation Detection\n    15→The system SHALL detect evidence of scenario implementation.\n    16→\n    17→Detection methods (in order of confidence):\n    18→1. Test file with `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"<id>\")` decorator\n    19→2. Code comment `# Verify: <scenario-id>`\n    20→3. Spec archive with \"implemented\" marker\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Find test marker\n    23→- **WHEN** a test file contains `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"session-protocol/first-time-bootstrap\")`\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 verify` marks that scenario as covered\n    25→\n    26→### Requirement: Coverage Report\n    27→The system SHALL generate a coverage report showing implemented vs missing scenarios.\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Incomplete implementation\n    30→- **WHEN** some scenarios have no detected implementation\n    31→- **THEN** `se3 verify` lists uncovered scenarios with file paths\n    32→- **AND** exits with code 1\n    33→\n    34→#### Scenario: Complete implementation\n    35→- **WHEN** all scenarios have detected implementation\n    36→- **THEN** `se3 verify` reports success\n    37→- **AND** exits with code 0\n    38→\n    39→### Requirement: Skip Annotation\n    40→The system SHALL support explicitly skipping scenarios.\n    41→\n    42→#### Scenario: Skip with reason\n    43→- **WHEN** a spec contains `<!-- verify-skip: reason -->` before a scenario\n    44→- **THEN** that scenario is excluded from coverage requirements\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"30615668-b911-408f-ba5b-097ae41ebe11"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_1Lj8CkbO0VuPE4UhQoCjQYub","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# git-worktree-collab Specification (v2 - Simplified)\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→Replace the experimental External Controller (v2) with a simplified architecture based on the proven bash orchestrator (v1). Remove unnecessary complexity: no daemon, no HTTP API, no MCP server, no real-time manager-worker communication.\n     6→\n     7→**Core principle**: Git worktree-based isolation + file-based async communication only.\n     8→\n     9→## Architecture Overview\n    10→\n    11→```\n    12→┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    13→│  Layer 1: Orchestrator (bash)                       │\n    14→│  - Event loop: spawn → wait → react                 │\n    15→│  - File system is the ONLY communication channel    │\n    16→│  - Zero AI token cost while idle                    │\n    17→├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n    18→│  Layer 2: Manager (claude -p)                       │\n    19→│  - Invoked on-demand when decisions needed          │\n    20→│  - Stateless: reads .collab/, writes decisions      │\n    21→│  - NO real-time communication with workers          │\n    22→├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n    23→│  Layer 3: Workers (claude -p)                       │\n    24→│  - Isolated in git worktrees                        │\n    25→│  - Independent full context window                  │\n    26→│  - Commit & exit — process exit = task complete     │\n    27→└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n    28→```\n    29→\n    30→## Key Design Decisions\n    31→\n    32→### 1. No Daemon, No API, No MCP\n    33→\n    34→**REMOVED from v2:**\n    35→- ❌ Python daemon process (`daemon.py`)\n    36→- ❌ HTTP API server (`api_server.py`)\n    37→- ❌ MCP server for manager-worker communication (`mcp-collab/server`)\n    38→- ❌ Real-time progress reporting\n    39→\n    40→**RATIONALE:**\n    41→- Bash orchestrator is sufficient — it spawns processes and waits for exit\n    42→- File system communication is simpler and more reliable\n    43→- Manager and Worker do NOT need to communicate during worker execution\n    44→- Worker progress is tracked via git commits, not real-time reports\n    45→\n    46→### 2. Async Communication Only\n    47→\n    48→**Communication pattern:**\n    49→```\n    50→Manager creates Task File ──► Worker reads Task File (at start)\n    51→                              Worker works independently\n    52→                              Worker writes result (git commit + exit)\n    53→Worker exit triggers ───────► Manager reads result (at end)\n    54→```\n    55→\n    56→**NO communication during worker execution:**\n    57→- Manager does not monitor worker progress\n    58→- Worker does not ask manager questions mid-task\n    59→- Worker blocks/writes human-calls/ directly if human needed\n    60→\n    61→**RATIONALE:**\n    62→- Git worktree paradigm: branch is isolation boundary\n    63→- Commit history is the progress indicator\n    64→- Human calls go directly to human-calls/, not through manager\n    65→\n    66→### 3. Simplified Health Monitoring\n    67→\n    68→**Worker health:**\n    69→- Process timeout via bash `timeout` command\n    70→- Git commit activity check (orchestrator polls periodically)\n    71→- NO heartbeat/MCP required\n    72→\n    73→**Manager health:**\n    74→- Timeout on `claude -p` invocation\n    75→- JSON validation with retry\n    76→\n    77→**Orchestrator health:**\n    78→- NO watchdog layer — orchestrator crash = session ends\n    79→- State persistence in `.collab/` allows manual resume\n    80→\n    81→## Requirements\n    82→\n    83→### Requirement: Three-Layer Architecture (Simplified)\n    84→\n    85→The system SHALL use a three-layer architecture with bash orchestrator, manager (claude -p), and workers (claude -p).\n    86→\n    87→**Orchestrator** (Layer 1):\n    88→- Pure bash script — no daemon, no resident process\n    89→- Spawns manager/worker via `claude -p`, waits for exit\n    90→- Reads task files, updates status, makes spawn decisions\n    91→- All state in `.collab/` files — orchestrator is stateless\n    92→\n    93→**Manager** (Layer 2):\n    94→- Invoked via `claude -p` for specific decisions only\n    95→- Reads `.collab/` state, returns JSON decision\n    96→- Does NOT communicate with running workers\n    97→- Exits after returning decision\n    98→\n    99→**Workers** (Layer 3):\n   100→- Each runs as independent `claude -p` in git worktree\n   101→- Full context window, no shared state pressure\n   102→- Commits to branch, exits when done (exit code = result)\n   103→- If blocked: write to `human-calls/`, exit with code 2\n   104→\n   105→#### Scenario: Event-driven orchestrator loop\n   106→- **WHEN** orchestrator starts\n   107→- **THEN** it spawns manager for initial planning\n   108→- **WHEN** manager returns task list\n   109→- **THEN** orchestrator spawns workers up to `max_parallel_workers`\n   110→- **WHEN** any worker exits\n   111→- **THEN** orchestrator spawns manager for review/failure-handling\n   112→- **WHEN** all tasks terminal\n   113→- **THEN** orchestrator exits\n   114→\n   115→#### Scenario: Stateless recovery\n   116→- **WHEN** orchestrator is restarted with `--resume`\n   117→- **THEN** it reads `.collab/config.json` and `.collab/tasks/*.json`\n   118→- **AND** resumes the event loop from current state\n   119→\n   120→---\n   121→\n   122→### Requirement: Task File Protocol (Unchanged)\n   123→\n   124→The system SHALL use JSON task files in `.collab/tasks/` as the single source of truth.\n   125→\n   126→**Task lifecycle**: `pending` → `in_progress` → (`done` | `failed` | `blocked` | `timeout`)\n   127→\n   128→**Task file format**: Same as v1 (see original spec for full schema)\n   129→\n   130→Key fields:\n   131→- `id`, `status`, `branch`, `worktree`\n   132→- `prompt`: Full worker prompt\n   133→- `worker_pid`: Set by orchestrator when spawning\n   134→- `worker_exit_code`: Set by orchestrator on exit\n   135→- `health.attempts`: Retry counter\n   136→- `health.timeout_minutes`: Timeout setting\n   137→\n   138→**Collaboration config** (`.collab/config.json`):\n   139→```json\n   140→{\n   141→  \"session_id\": \"collab-20260215-100000\",\n   142→  \"objective\": \"Implement features X, Y, Z\",\n   143→  \"base_branch\": \"master\",\n   144→  \"max_parallel_workers\": 3,\n   145→  \"worker_timeout_minutes\": 60,\n   146→  \"manager_timeout_minutes\": 15,\n   147→  \"status\": \"active\"\n   148→}\n   149→```\n   150→\n   151→#### Scenario: Task persistence\n   152→- **WHEN** orchestrator spawns a worker\n   153→- **THEN** it writes `worker_pid` to task file\n   154→- **WHEN** worker exits\n   155→- **THEN** orchestrator records `worker_exit_code` and updates `status`\n   156→\n   157→---\n   158→\n   159→### Requirement: Manager-Worker Async Boundary\n   160→\n   161→Manager and Worker SHALL NOT communicate during worker execution.\n   162→\n   163→**Worker prompt includes:**\n   164→- Task description\n   165→- Spec references\n   166→- Rules (from `rules-worker.md`)\n   167→- Instruction: \"Work independently. Commit your changes. Exit when done.\"\n   168→\n   169→**Worker blocked/needs help:**\n   170→- Worker writes to `human-calls/` directly\n   171→- Worker exits with code 2 (blocked)\n   172→- Orchestrator sets status to `blocked`\n   173→- Human response processed in next orchestrator loop\n   174→\n   175→**Manager decisions (JSON response):**\n   176→```json\n   177→{\n   178→  \"action\": \"plan|merge|reject|retry|split|escalate|complete\",\n   179→  \"tasks\": [...],\n   180→  \"target_task\": \"task-001\",\n   181→  \"merge_branch\": \"collab/feature-x\",\n   182→  \"retry_prompt\": \"...\",\n   183→  \"reason\": \"...\",\n   184→  \"summary\": \"...\"\n   185→}\n   186→```\n   187→\n   188→#### Scenario: Worker blocked on ambiguity\n   189→- **WHEN** worker encounters unclear requirement\n   190→- **THEN** worker writes `human-calls/task-001-clarification.md`\n   191→- **AND** worker exits with code 2\n   192→- **WHEN** orchestrator sees exit code 2\n   193→- **THEN** sets status to `blocked` and continues other tasks\n   194→- **WHEN** human responds\n   195→- **THEN** orchestrator re-spawns worker with human's answer\n   196→\n   197→#### Scenario: Manager reviews completed work\n   198→- **WHEN** worker exits with code 0\n   199→- **THEN** orchestrator invokes manager with review context\n   200→- **AND** manager examines git diff (not live worker)\n   201→- **THEN** manager returns merge/reject/split decision\n   202→\n   203→---\n   204→\n   205→### Requirement: Orchestrator Event Loop (Bash)\n   206→\n   207→The orchestrator SHALL implement a simple bash event loop.\n   208→\n   209→```bash\n   210→# Pseudocode\n   211→load_state() {\n   212→  CONFIG=$(cat .collab/config.json)\n   213→  TASKS=(.collab/tasks/task-*.json)\n   214→}\n   215→\n   216→spawn_manager(event_type, context) {\n   217→  claude -p \"$MANAGER_PROMPT\" --output-format json\n   218→  # Parse JSON, return action\n   219→}\n   220→\n   221→spawn_worker(task_id) {\n   222→  git worktree add \".worktrees/$task_id\" -b \"collab/$task_id\"\n   223→  claude -p \"$WORKER_PROMPT\" &\n   224→  echo $! > .collab/tasks/$task_id.pid\n   225→}\n   226→\n   227→main_loop() {\n   228→  load_state\n   229→\n   230→  # Initial planning if no tasks\n   231→  if no_tasks; then\n   232→    action=$(spawn_manager \"plan\" \"$OBJECTIVE\")\n   233→    create_task_files \"$action\"\n   234→  fi\n   235→\n   236→  # Main loop\n   237→  while true; do\n   238→    # Spawn workers up to limit\n   239→    for task in pending_tasks; do\n   240→      if active_workers < max_parallel; then\n   241→        spawn_worker \"$task\"\n   242→      fi\n   243→    done\n   244→\n   245→    # Wait for any child to exit\n   246→    wait -n  # bash waits for any background job\n   247→\n   248→    # Process exited workers\n   249→    for task in in_progress_tasks; do\n   250→      if ! kill -0 \"$task.pid\" 2>/dev/null; then\n   251→        exit_code=$?  # Get actual exit code\n   252→        handle_worker_exit \"$task\" \"$exit_code\"\n   253→      fi\n   254→    done\n   255→\n   256→    # Check if all tasks terminal\n   257→    if all_terminal; then\n   258→      generate_summary\n   259→      exit 0\n   260→    fi\n   261→  done\n   262→}\n   263→```\n   264→\n   265→#### Scenario: Parallel worker spawning\n   266→- **WHEN** 3 tasks pending and max_parallel=3\n   267→- **THEN** orchestrator spawns all 3 workers concurrently\n   268→- **WHEN** one worker exits\n   269→- **THEN** orchestrator spawns manager for review\n   270→- **AND** may spawn next pending worker if slot available\n   271→\n   272→#### Scenario: Sequential manager invocation\n   273→- **WHEN** two workers complete simultaneously\n   274→- **THEN** orchestrator queues manager invocations\n   275→- **AND** processes them one at a time\n   276→\n   277→---\n   278→\n   279→### Requirement: Health Monitoring (Simplified)\n   280→\n   281→**Worker timeout:**\n   282→- Bash `timeout` command wraps worker: `timeout ${TIMEOUT}m claude -p ...`\n   283→- Exit code 124 = timeout\n   284→\n   285→**Stall detection:**\n   286→- Orchestrator periodically checks: `git -C \"$worktree\" log -1 --format=%ct`\n   287→- No commit within `stale_threshold_minutes` = stall\n   288→- Kill worker, set status to `timeout`\n   289→\n   290→**Manager timeout:**\n   291→- `timeout ${MANAGER_TIMEOUT}m claude -p ...`\n   292→- On timeout: retry once with simplified prompt\n   293→- On retry failure: escalate to human\n   294→\n   295→#### Scenario: Worker timeout\n   296→- **WHEN** worker exceeds `worker_timeout_minutes`\n   297→- **THEN** `timeout` kills the process, exit code 124\n   298→- **AND** orchestrator sets status to `timeout`\n   299→- **AND** invokes manager for retry/escalate decision\n   300→\n   301→#### Scenario: Worker stall (no commits)\n   302→- **WHEN** worker alive but no git activity for 30 minutes\n   303→- **THEN** orchestrator kills worker\n   304→- **AND** sets status to `timeout`\n   305→\n   306→---\n   307→\n   308→### Requirement: Human-as-MCP (Direct)\n   309→\n   310→Workers and Manager SHALL interact with human directly via `human-calls/` directory.\n   311→\n   312→**Worker blocked:**\n   313→1. Worker writes `human-calls/{task-id}-{question}.md`\n   314→2. Worker exits with code 2\n   315→3. Orchestrator sets task status to `blocked`\n   316→4. Orchestrator continues other tasks\n   317→5. When human responds, orchestrator re-spawns worker\n   318→\n   319→**Manager escalates:**\n   320→1. Manager writes `human-calls/escalate-{timestamp}.md`\n   321→2. Manager returns `{\"action\": \"escalate\", ...}`\n   322→3. Orchestrator pauses session, waits for human\n   323→\n   324→**NO MCP server required** — direct file I/O.\n   325→\n   326→---\n   327→\n   328→## CLI Commands\n   329→\n   330→```bash\n   331→# Start automatic collaboration\n   332→se3 collab --daemon \"Implement auth and caching\"\n   333→\n   334→# Resume crashed session\n   335→se3 collab --resume\n   336→\n   337→# Check status\n   338→se3 collab --status\n   339→\n   340→# Abort and cleanup\n   341→se3 collab --abort\n   342→\n   343→# Manual mode (generate plan, execute manually)\n   344→se3 collab --manual \"Implement feature X\"\n   345→```\n   346→\n   347→## Configuration (se3.config.yaml)\n   348→\n   349→```yaml\n   350→collab:\n   351→  max_parallel_workers: 3\n   352→  worker_timeout_minutes: 60\n   353→  manager_timeout_minutes: 15\n   354→  stale_threshold_minutes: 30\n   355→  max_worker_attempts: 3\n   356→  max_manager_retries: 2\n   357→```\n   358→\n   359→## Design Principle: Minimize External Dependencies\n   360→\n   361→The orchestrator layer (bash scripts) SHALL minimize external tool dependencies. Python 3 is the only required runtime beyond standard POSIX tools.\n   362→\n   363→**Rationale:**\n   364→- Collab runs in diverse environments; every additional `apt install` raises the barrier to use\n   365→- SE3's tech stack is Python — reuse it rather than adding system-level dependencies\n   366→\n   367→**Current application — `jq-complete.py`:**\n   368→- Shell scripts use `$JQ_CMD` which auto-detects system `jq`, falling back to `scripts/jq-complete.py`\n   369→- The Python fallback covers the subset of jq used by the orchestrator (field access, defaults, assignment)\n   370→- It is NOT a full jq replacement — complex expressions like `select()`, `map()`, `if-then-else` are unsupported\n   371→\n   372→**Future direction:**\n   373→- When JSON processing logic in shell scripts grows complex enough to need advanced jq features, migrate that logic to Python modules instead of extending `jq-complete.py`\n   374→- Shell scripts should stay as thin orchestration glue; non-trivial data processing belongs in Python\n   375→\n   376→---\n   377→\n   378→## Migration from v2 (External Controller)\n   379→\n   380→**To be removed:**\n   381→- `tools/se3_tools/controller/` (daemon, api_server, persistence)\n   382→- `tools/se3_tools/commands/collab_v2.py`\n   383→- `scripts/mcp-collab/`\n   384→\n   385→**To keep:**\n   386→- `tools/se3_tools/commands/collab.py` (v1 bash orchestrator)\n   387→- `scripts/collab-orchestrator.sh`\n   388→- `scripts/rules-worker.md`\n   389→- `scripts/rules-manager.md`\n   390→\n   391→**Behavior change:**\n   392→- No `se3 controller` commands\n   393→- No persistent daemon process\n   394→- `--daemon` flag starts bash orchestrator in background (not Python daemon)\n   395→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"09636f54-6b43-4a3e-883d-f0705a9d66b5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_Z2mOwgKeyS0XUuaEAmiBirAq","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# agent-team Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the multi-agent task coordination system using Claude Code's native Task tool. This spec governs how parent agents distribute work to sub-agents, how role differentiation works through prompts, and how change-level isolation enables safe parallel execution.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Multi-Agent Task Coordination\n     7→The system SHALL support multi-agent parallel work using Claude Code's native Task tool with sub-agents.\n     8→\n     9→Parent agents distribute work by spawning sub-agents via `Task` tool with appropriate `subagent_type`. Sub-agents operate on the same file system and return results directly to the parent. No file-based communication channel is needed.\n    10→\n    11→Isolation is achieved through openspec changes — each sub-agent works on a different change, naturally touching different files.\n    12→\n    13→#### Scenario: Task distribution via native Task tool\n    14→- **WHEN** a project has multiple independent openspec changes to implement\n    15→- **THEN** the parent agent spawns sub-agents via Task tool, each assigned a different change\n    16→\n    17→#### Scenario: Conflict avoidance\n    18→- **WHEN** multiple sub-agents work in parallel\n    19→- **THEN** change-level isolation ensures agents do not modify the same files simultaneously\n    20→\n    21→### Requirement: Agent Role Differentiation\n    22→The system SHALL support agent role differentiation through Task tool prompts.\n    23→\n    24→Roles are expressed in the prompt given to sub-agents, not through separate configuration files:\n    25→- **architect**: Responsible for spec design, change proposals, architecture decisions\n    26→- **implementer**: Implements code according to specs and design\n    27→- **reviewer**: Verifies implementation matches specs\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Role assignment via prompt\n    30→- **WHEN** a parent agent spawns a sub-agent for implementation work\n    31→- **THEN** the prompt specifies the role (e.g., \"As an implementer, execute tasks 1-3 of change X\")\n    32→\n    33→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"58994864-f7b0-487a-84c1-3b27aa990a1f"}
