{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_bcW7NYVS8SdaerViQcMzhEW4","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# output-sync Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Manages the `output/` directory which contains templates for `se3 init`. With the SE3 module system (v7.0), runtime file sync was removed — `output/` only holds templates and documentation.\n     5→\n     6→## Requirements\n     7→### Requirement: Template Management\n     8→The system SHALL manage template files in `output/` for use by `se3 init`.\n     9→\n    10→Templates:\n    11→- `output/SE3.md.template` → `.claude/SE3.md`\n    12→- `output/CLAUDE.minimal.md.template` → `.claude/CLAUDE.md`\n    13→- `output/status.md.template` → `status.md`\n    14→\n    15→#### Scenario: List templates\n    16→- **WHEN** `se3 sync --dry-run` is executed\n    17→- **THEN** it lists template files and their status\n    18→\n    19→### Requirement: Orphan Detection\n    20→The system SHALL detect output files that are not recognized templates.\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Orphaned output file\n    23→- **WHEN** an output file exists that is not a recognized template\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 sync` warns about the orphaned file\n    25→- **AND** `--prune` flag removes orphaned files\n    26→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"305229ce-478c-468a-954c-c251e42669f3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_NMPzmL2P6n5z6DRqSrpIbjD9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# se3-config Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the SE 3.0 framework configuration system via `se3.config.yaml`. This spec governs configurable parameters, default values, and how the framework adapts its behavior based on project-specific settings.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Configuration File Format\n     7→The system SHALL support configuring framework behavior via `se3.config.yaml`.\n     8→\n     9→The configuration file is located at the project root using YAML format. All configuration items MUST have sensible defaults.\n    10→\n    11→The system SHALL also support a global config at `~/.se3/config.yaml`. Project-level config overrides global config at the top-level key level (no deep merge).\n    12→\n    13→Configurable options include:\n    14→- `max_tasks_per_change`: Maximum number of tasks per change (default: 5)\n    15→- `human_call.timeout_days`: Default timeout days for human calls (default: 7)\n    16→- `human_call.language`: Language for human call messages (e.g., `zh-CN`, `en-US`)\n    17→- `session.max_progress_entries`: Maximum session records to retain in progress (default: 20)\n    18→- `commit.test_command`: Custom test command for `se3 commit` (default: auto-detect pytest)\n    19→- `claude_commands`: List of `{cmd, priority}` entries for Claude command resolution with priority-based fallback (default: `[{cmd: \"claude\", priority: 0}]`)\n    20→\n    21→#### Scenario: Using default configuration\n    22→- **WHEN** no se3.config.yaml file exists in the project\n    23→- **THEN** the framework runs with built-in default values\n    24→\n    25→#### Scenario: Custom configuration\n    26→- **WHEN** se3.config.yaml exists and specifies max_tasks_per_change as 3\n    27→- **THEN** the framework limits each change to maximum 3 tasks when creating changes\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Global configuration\n    30→- **WHEN** `~/.se3/config.yaml` exists with `claude_commands` and no project-level config exists\n    31→- **THEN** the framework uses the global `claude_commands` list\n    32→\n    33→#### Scenario: Project overrides global\n    34→- **WHEN** both global and project configs define `claude_commands`\n    35→- **THEN** the project-level `claude_commands` replaces the global list entirely\n    36→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"f2628793-6eaa-4f99-9337-a59f15f7f78b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_VBWKcrBxrZHzojGDmPWz9E76","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# requirement-intake Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change requirement-capture. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Three-Source Requirement Intake\n     7→The system SHALL accept new requirements from three distinct sources and route them through a unified `openspec change` creation process.\n     8→\n     9→**Source 1: Autonomous Discovery (Agent-Initiated)**\n    10→- Trigger: Agent identifies missing capability during implementation\n    11→- Flow: Agent creates new openspec change directly → proposal captures the discovered requirement\n    12→- Marker: `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` in proposal\n    13→\n    14→**Source 2: Human-MCP Call (Requested)**\n    15→- Trigger: Agent issues human call asking for requirements input\n    16→- Flow: Human responds → Agent parses response → Creates openspec change\n    17→- Marker: `[Source: human-mcp]` in proposal\n    18→\n    19→**Source 3: Human-Initiated (External)**\n    20→- Trigger: Human provides new requirement through any interaction (at natural conversation boundary or during active session)\n    21→- Flow: Agent recognizes human-initiated input → Creates new openspec change\n    22→- Marker: `[Source: human-initiated]` in proposal\n    23→- Note: \"Human-initiated\" is about **who originated the requirement** (human, not agent), not **how it was delivered** (interrupt or natural turn). All human-proposed requirements go through this path.\n    24→\n    25→#### Scenario: Autonomous discovery during implementation\n    26→- **WHEN** an agent realizes a needed feature wasn't in the original spec during coding\n    27→- **THEN** the agent creates a new openspec change with `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` marker\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Human provides requirements via MCP response\n    30→- **WHEN** a human fills in the Response section of a requirements-request call\n    31→- **THEN** the agent parses it and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-mcp]` marker\n    32→\n    33→#### Scenario: Human provides requirement through any interaction\n    34→- **WHEN** a human provides a new requirement (whether at natural conversation boundary or by interrupting active session)\n    35→- **THEN** the agent recognizes this as human-initiated input and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-initiated]` marker\n    36→- **NOTE** The distinction between \"interrupt\" and \"natural turn\" is not functionally significant; both are human-initiated requirements\n    37→\n    38→### Requirement: Unified Change Creation\n    39→All three intake sources SHALL result in the same downstream process: an openspec change with proposal → specs → implementation.\n    40→\n    41→#### Scenario: Consistent processing regardless of source\n    42→- **WHEN** any of the three sources triggers\n    43→- **THEN** the resulting openspec change follows the same SDD workflow (proposal → specs → design → tasks → code → verify → archive)\n    44→\n    45→### Requirement: Interruption Context Preservation\n    46→When Source 3 (human interrupt) occurs, the system SHALL preserve context of interrupted work.\n    47→\n    48→**Context preservation options:**\n    49→1. **Stack-based**: Push current change to stack, create new interrupt change, pop on completion\n    50→2. **Link-based**: New change references \"interrupted from: <change-id>\"\n    51→3. **Checkpoint-based**: Mark checkpoint in current change, resume from there after interrupt\n    52→\n    53→#### Scenario: Resume after interrupt\n    54→- **WHEN** a human-interrupt change completes\n    55→- **THEN** agent asks: \"Resume previous work on `<change-id>`? [Y/n]\"\n    56→\n    57→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"3c4794c7-e7fe-4a8e-9f10-f9a090a89ac6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_nLbtG1YCBXiQI11xl7YxoaZR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→openapi: 3.0.0\n     2→info:\n     3→  title: SE3 External Controller\n     4→  version: 1.0.0\n     5→  description: |\n     6→    External control daemon for SE3 framework.\n     7→    Manages Claude sessions, auto-commit, and multi-agent collaboration.\n     8→\n     9→paths:\n    10→  # Session Management\n    11→  /session/start:\n    12→    post:\n    13→      summary: Start new interactive session\n    14→      requestBody:\n    15→        content:\n    16→          application/json:\n    17→            schema:\n    18→              type: object\n    19→              properties:\n    20→                objective:\n    21→                  type: string\n    22→                mode:\n    23→                  type: string\n    24→                  enum: [interactive, worker, manager]\n    25→      responses:\n    26→        200:\n    27→          description: Session started\n    28→          content:\n    29→            application/json:\n    30→              schema:\n    31→                type: object\n    32→                properties:\n    33→                  session_id:\n    34→                    type: string\n    35→                  pid:\n    36→                    type: integer\n    37→\n    38→  /session/stop:\n    39→    post:\n    40→      summary: Stop session with commit\n    41→      requestBody:\n    42→        content:\n    43→          application/json:\n    44→            schema:\n    45→              type: object\n    46→              properties:\n    47→                force:\n    48→                  type: boolean\n    49→                skip_commit:\n    50→                  type: boolean\n    51→      responses:\n    52→        200:\n    53→          description: Session stopped\n    54→\n    55→  /session/status:\n    56→    get:\n    57→      summary: Get current session status\n    58→      responses:\n    59→        200:\n    60→          description: Session status\n    61→          content:\n    62→            application/json:\n    63→              schema:\n    64→                type: object\n    65→                properties:\n    66→                  session_id:\n    67→                    type: string\n    68→                  status:\n    69→                    type: string\n    70→                    enum: [active, paused, stopping]\n    71→                  pending_commits:\n    72→                    type: boolean\n    73→\n    74→  # Auto-Commit Control\n    75→  /commit/trigger:\n    76→    post:\n    77→      summary: Trigger immediate commit\n    78→      requestBody:\n    79→        content:\n    80→          application/json:\n    81→            schema:\n    82→              type: object\n    83→              properties:\n    84→                reason:\n    85→                  type: string\n    86→                message:\n    87→                  type: string\n    88→      responses:\n    89→        200:\n    90→          description: Commit result\n    91→\n    92→  /commit/config:\n    93→    get:\n    94→      summary: Get auto-commit configuration\n    95→      responses:\n    96→        200:\n    97→          description: Configuration\n    98→    put:\n    99→      summary: Update auto-commit configuration\n   100→      requestBody:\n   101→        content:\n   102→          application/json:\n   103→            schema:\n   104→              type: object\n   105→              properties:\n   106→                silence_timeout:\n   107→                  type: integer\n   108→                  description: Seconds of inactivity before auto-commit\n   109→                commit_on_test_pass:\n   110→                  type: boolean\n   111→                commit_on_session_end:\n   112→                  type: boolean\n   113→\n   114→  # Collaboration\n   115→  /collab/start:\n   116→    post:\n   117→      summary: Start collaboration session\n   118→      requestBody:\n   119→        content:\n   120→          application/json:\n   121→            schema:\n   122→              type: object\n   123→              properties:\n   124→                objective:\n   125→                  type: string\n   126→                max_workers:\n   127→                  type: integer\n   128→      responses:\n   129→        200:\n   130→          description: Collaboration started\n   131→\n   132→  /collab/spawn:\n   133→    post:\n   134→      summary: Spawn worker/manager process\n   135→      requestBody:\n   136→        content:\n   137→          application/json:\n   138→            schema:\n   139→              type: object\n   140→              properties:\n   141→                role:\n   142→                  type: string\n   143→                  enum: [worker, manager]\n   144→                task_id:\n   145→                  type: string\n   146→                prompt:\n   147→                  type: string\n   148→      responses:\n   149→        200:\n   150→          description: Process spawned\n   151→\n   152→components:\n   153→  schemas:\n   154→    Session:\n   155→      type: object\n   156→      properties:\n   157→        session_id:\n   158→          type: string\n   159→        pid:\n   160→          type: integer\n   161→        mode:\n   162→          type: string\n   163→        start_time:\n   164→          type: string\n   165→          format: date-time\n   166→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"602e8e49-902d-4884-ad1d-71103b4157d5"}
