{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_obIiiBTtEAfuO7AWcQJM9VWn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# SE3 Module System Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→\n     5→The SE3 Module System defines the architecture for separating framework-level conventions from project-specific configurations in SE 3.0. It establishes clear boundaries between:\n     6→\n     7→1. **Framework layer**: Core SE 3.0 conventions and behaviors\n     8→2. **Project layer**: Project-specific customizations and configurations\n     9→\n    10→This separation ensures consistency across SE 3.0 projects while allowing teams to tailor conventions to their specific needs.\n    11→\n    12→## Requirements\n    13→\n    14→### Requirement: Project Configuration File Structure\n    15→\n    16→All SE 3.0 projects SHALL have the following configuration file structure:\n    17→\n    18→```\n    19→project/\n    20→└── .claude/\n    21→    ├── CLAUDE.md    # Project-specific conventions (required)\n    22→    └── SE3.md       # Framework conventions (required, managed by se3 tool)\n    23→```\n    24→\n    25→#### Scenario: New project initialization\n    26→- **WHEN** a new SE 3.0 project is initialized\n    27→- **THEN** it SHALL have a CLAUDE.md file with project-specific content\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Detecting redundant content\n    30→- **WHEN** CLAUDE.md contains duplicate content from SE3.md\n    31→- **THEN** `se3 lint` SHALL warn about redundancy\n    32→\n    33→### Requirement: CLAUDE.md (Project-Specific Conventions)\n    34→\n    35→The project-specific CLAUDE.md file SHALL define project-specific conventions, workflows, and guardrails that supplement or override framework defaults.\n    36→\n    37→- CLAUDE.md SHALL exist in every SE 3.0 project\n    38→- CLAUDE.md SHALL be a valid Markdown file\n    39→- CLAUDE.md SHALL contain project-specific configuration (cannot be empty)\n    40→- CLAUDE.md SHALL NOT duplicate content from SE3.md unnecessarily\n    41→- CLAUDE.md MAY override framework conventions with project-specific rules\n    42→- CLAUDE.md SHALL be maintained by the project team\n    43→\n    44→#### Scenario: Modifying project conventions\n    45→- **WHEN** the project team updates CLAUDE.md\n    46→- **THEN** the changes SHALL take effect for all subsequent se3 commands\n    47→\n    48→### Requirement: SE3.md (Framework Conventions)\n    49→\n    50→The SE3.md file SHALL provide the core SE 3.0 framework conventions that apply to all projects.\n    51→\n    52→- SE3.md SHALL exist in every SE 3.0 project\n    53→- SE3.md SHALL be a valid Markdown file\n    54→- SE3.md SHALL contain the official SE 3.0 framework conventions\n    55→- SE3.md SHALL NOT be modified manually by project teams\n    56→- SE3.md SHALL be managed exclusively through the se3 tool commands\n    57→- SE3.md SHALL include version metadata for checksum validation\n    58→\n    59→#### Scenario: Updating framework conventions\n    60→- **WHEN** `se3 update` is run\n    61→- **THEN** SE3.md SHALL be updated to the latest version\n    62→\n    63→### Requirement: se3 init Command Behavior\n    64→\n    65→The se3 init command SHALL initialize a new or existing project as an SE 3.0 project.\n    66→\n    67→- se3 init SHALL create the .claude directory if it does not exist\n    68→- se3 init SHALL create CLAUDE.md if it does not exist\n    69→- se3 init SHALL create SE3.md if it does not exist\n    70→- se3 init SHALL populate CLAUDE.md with a project-specific template\n    71→- se3 init SHALL populate SE3.md with the latest framework conventions\n    72→- se3 init SHALL generate and store a checksum for SE3.md\n    73→- se3 init SHALL NOT overwrite existing files unless --force option is used\n    74→- se3 init SHALL provide a --force option to overwrite existing files\n    75→\n    76→#### Scenario: Initializing a new project\n    77→- **WHEN** se3 init is run on a non-SE3 project\n    78→- **THEN** it SHALL create .claude/CLAUDE.md and .claude/SE3.md\n    79→\n    80→#### Scenario: Existing project initialization\n    81→- **WHEN** se3 init is run on an existing SE3 project without --force\n    82→- **THEN** it SHALL NOT modify existing files\n    83→\n    84→#### Scenario: Forced initialization\n    85→- **WHEN** se3 init is run with --force\n    86→- **THEN** it SHALL overwrite existing configuration files\n    87→\n    88→### Requirement: se3 update Command Behavior\n    89→\n    90→The `se3 update` command SHALL update the SE 3.0 framework conventions in an existing project.\n    91→\n    92→- `se3 update` SHALL regenerate SE3.md from the installed framework template\n    93→- `se3 update` SHALL compare local SE3.md version with latest available\n    94→- `se3 update` SHALL update the checksum for the new SE3.md\n    95→- `se3 update` SHALL preserve CLAUDE.md (project-specific configuration)\n    96→- `se3 update` SHALL display a summary of changes during update\n    97→\n    98→#### Scenario: Updating to latest version\n    99→- **WHEN** `se3 update` is run and a newer version exists\n   100→- **THEN** it SHALL replace SE3.md with the latest version\n   101→\n   102→#### Scenario: Already on latest version\n   103→- **WHEN** `se3 update` is run and already on latest version\n   104→- **THEN** it SHALL display \"Already on latest version\" message\n   105→\n   106→## Verification Tests\n   107→\n   108→To verify the module system functionality:\n   109→\n   110→1. **Initialization Test**:\n   111→   ```bash\n   112→   mkdir test-project && cd test-project\n   113→   se3 init\n   114→   ls -la .claude/  # Should contain CLAUDE.md and SE3.md\n   115→   ```\n   116→\n   117→2. **Update Test**:\n   118→   ```bash\n   119→   se3 update  # Should check and update to latest version\n   120→   ```\n   121→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"901a1359-4c4d-4b18-85f2-4ffa9b8bcca6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_P65rN9AfbVnZiE44F4Aw6UEF","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# se3-scaffold Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the SE 3.0 project scaffold system, including the CLAUDE.md + SE3.md template system, standard project structure, configuration system, and self-iterate workflow. This spec governs how SE 3.0 generates its output artifacts and how new projects adopt the framework.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: CLAUDE.md + SE3.md Template System\n     7→The system SHALL produce a two-part template system:\n     8→1. **CLAUDE.md** - Minimal project-level configuration file with core framework references\n     9→2. **SE3.md** - Complete framework implementation with all process definitions\n    10→\n    11→The **CLAUDE.md template MUST include**:\n    12→- Core principles references\n    13→- Session protocol references\n    14→- Minimal project structure specification\n    15→- Reference to SE3.md for complete framework documentation\n    16→\n    17→The **SE3.md template MUST include**:\n    18→- Complete standard process definitions (startup, execution, shutdown protocols)\n    19→- Detailed special file specifications (status.md, progress.md, human-calls/, etc.)\n    20→- Full conventional behavior definitions (self-iterate, change management, etc.)\n    21→- Human-as-MCP invocation specifications\n    22→- Agent Team collaboration specifications\n    23→- Verification protocol\n    24→- Spec guardrails\n    25→- SDD process\n    26→- Configuration system details\n    27→\n    28→#### Scenario: New project adopts SE 3.0\n    29→- **WHEN** a user initializes SE 3.0 framework in a new project\n    30→- **THEN** the system generates both CLAUDE.md (minimal) and SE3.md (complete) in the .claude/ directory\n    31→\n    32→### Requirement: SE 3.0 Project Structure\n    33→The system SHALL define the standard SE 3.0 project file structure.\n    34→\n    35→Standard structure:\n    36→```\n    37→project/\n    38→├── init.sh                # Environment setup (optional)\n    39→├── status.md              # Runtime dashboard (current session state)\n    40→├── progress.md            # Cross-session progress tracking\n    41→├── se3.config.yaml        # Framework configuration (optional)\n    42→├── README.md              # Project documentation\n    43→├── human-calls/           # Async human call queue\n    44→├── openspec/\n    45→│   ├── specs/             # Source of truth for requirements\n    46→│   └── changes/\n    47→│       └── archive/\n    48→└── .claude/\n    49→    ├── CLAUDE.md          # SE 3.0 minimal framework reference (project-level)\n    50→    └── SE3.md             # Complete SE 3.0 framework implementation\n    51→```\n    52→\n    53→OpenSpec specs serve as the single source of truth for project requirements. No separate demands/requirements file is needed.\n    54→\n    55→#### Scenario: Project initialization\n    56→- **WHEN** SE 3.0 is initialized in a directory\n    57→- **THEN** the standard file structure is created with both CLAUDE.md and SE3.md\n    58→\n    59→### Requirement: Configuration System\n    60→The system SHALL support configuring framework behavior via `se3.config.yaml`.\n    61→\n    62→Configuration options include:\n    63→- `max_tasks_per_change`: Maximum tasks per change (default: 5)\n    64→- `human_call.timeout_days`: Default timeout days for human calls (default: 7)\n    65→- `agent_team.roles`: List of enabled agent roles\n    66→- `session.max_progress_entries`: Maximum session records to keep in progress (default: 20)\n    67→\n    68→#### Scenario: Using default configuration\n    69→- **WHEN** no se3.config.yaml file exists in the project\n    70→- **THEN** the framework runs with built-in default values\n    71→\n    72→### Requirement: SE3.md Generation via se3 init\n    73→The system SHALL generate SE3.md file via the `se3 init` command.\n    74→\n    75→The `se3 init` command MUST:\n    76→1. Create the .claude/ directory if it doesn't exist\n    77→2. Generate SE3.md with the complete framework implementation\n    78→3. Generate CLAUDE.md with minimal framework references\n    79→4. Ensure both files are properly formatted\n    80→5. Preserve existing files if they already exist\n    81→\n    82→#### Scenario: SE3.md generation on initialization\n    83→- **WHEN** a user runs `se3 init` in a project directory\n    84→- **THEN** the system creates .claude/SE3.md with the complete framework and .claude/CLAUDE.md with minimal content\n    85→\n    86→### Requirement: Output Artifacts\n    87→The system SHALL produce the following deliverables:\n    88→1. Project-level CLAUDE.md template (minimal, English)\n    89→2. Project-level SE3.md template (complete, English)\n    90→3. Configuration file template\n    91→4. Documentation and best practices guide\n    92→5. CLI tools documentation (TOOLS.md)\n    93→\n    94→#### Scenario: Complete delivery\n    95→- **WHEN** SE 3.0 framework design is complete\n    96→- **THEN** all deliverables are available for direct use in new projects\n    97→\n    98→#### Scenario: Backward compatibility\n    99→- **WHEN** a project has an existing CLAUDE.md without SE3.md\n   100→- **THEN** the framework continues to function with the existing CLAUDE.md file\n   101→\n   102→### Requirement: CLI Tools\n   103→The system SHALL provide CLI tools for validating and enforcing SE 3.0 conventions.\n   104→\n   105→Tools include:\n   106→- `se3 init` — Initialize a new SE 3.0 project\n   107→- `se3 lint` — Validate spec file format and content\n   108→- `se3 sync` — Synchronize output/ directory with source files\n   109→- `se3 verify` — Verify change implementation covers all spec scenarios\n   110→- `se3 status` — Diagnose session state and identify issues\n   111→- `se3 update` — Update SE3.md to the latest framework version\n   112→- `se3 commit` — Commit changes with test verification and sensitive file checks\n   113→- `se3 collab` — Manage git-worktree multi-agent collaboration\n   114→- `se3 claude-cmd` — Show configured Claude commands by priority\n   115→\n   116→#### Scenario: Spec validation\n   117→- **WHEN** a developer runs `se3 lint`\n   118→- **THEN** the tool validates all specs and reports any format violations\n   119→\n   120→#### Scenario: Change verification\n   121→- **WHEN** an agent completes implementing a change\n   122→- **THEN** `se3 verify --change <name>` confirms all scenarios are covered before archiving\n   123→\n   124→### Requirement: Self-Iterate Flow\n   125→The system SHALL define a self-iterate behavior that drives the project from human intent to working implementation.\n   126→\n   127→Flow:\n   128→1. Obtain direction via human call → create openspec change (proposal captures the intent)\n   129→2. Implement the change (specs → design → tasks → code)\n   130→3. Verify implementation against specs\n   131→4. Check if specs fully cover the project goals — if gaps exist, go to 1\n   132→5. Update project documentation\n   133→\n   134→#### Scenario: Self-iterate execution\n   135→- **WHEN** agent is instructed to self-iterate\n   136→- **THEN** agent executes the flow without stopping until step 5, using human calls only when blocked\n   137→\n   138→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"2754abdb-0436-4e66-bcf8-28099f378073"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_XWUqIKLoL3Jq4z0v8NA6tK9X","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# session-protocol Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the session lifecycle protocol for SE 3.0 agents. This spec governs progressive startup (se3 status → progress.md → scope determination), execution boundaries, shutdown procedures, and tool-enforced progress tracking across sessions.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Session Startup Protocol\n     7→The system SHALL define a progressive session startup protocol. The agent MUST locate current state with minimal context, then load more on demand.\n     8→\n     9→Startup steps:\n    10→1. Verify `openspec` CLI is available; if not, ask the human to install it. If available but `openspec/` directory does not exist, run `openspec init`.\n    11→2. Run `se3 status` for computed project state (git, collab, human-calls)\n    12→3. Read `head -50 progress.md` + `git log --oneline -5`\n    13→4. Scan `human-calls/` for responded but unprocessed requests\n    14→5. Check `openspec/changes/` for active changes and `openspec/specs/` for current capabilities\n    15→6. Determine session scope based on progress \"next steps\" + active changes\n    16→7. Load additional files only when the task requires them\n    17→\n    18→If step 3 finds no progress.md and no git history → **first-time bootstrap**:\n    19→- Ask the human (sync human call): \"What should this project do?\"\n    20→- Create an openspec change from the response (proposal captures the intent)\n    21→- Create `progress.md`\n    22→\n    23→#### Scenario: OpenSpec not installed\n    24→- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` command is not found\n    25→- **THEN** agent asks the human to install it via sync human call and does not proceed with spec-related work until resolved\n    26→\n    27→#### Scenario: OpenSpec not initialized\n    28→- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` is available but `openspec/` directory does not exist\n    29→- **THEN** agent runs `openspec init` before proceeding\n    30→\n    31→#### Scenario: Mature project startup\n    32→- **WHEN** agent starts a session with existing progress.md and git history\n    33→- **THEN** agent runs `se3 status`, reads progress.md and git log, then determines scope without reading all spec files upfront\n    34→\n    35→#### Scenario: First-time bootstrap\n    36→- **WHEN** agent starts a session with no progress.md and no git history\n    37→- **THEN** agent asks the human what to build via human call, creates an openspec change from the response\n    38→\n    39→#### Scenario: On-demand deep loading\n    40→- **WHEN** agent needs details about a specific capability during execution\n    41→- **THEN** agent reads the relevant spec file at that point, not during startup\n    42→\n    43→### Requirement: Session Execution Boundary\n    44→Each session MUST focus on a limited scope of work and MUST NOT attempt to complete too many tasks in a single session.\n    45→\n    46→#### Scenario: Session scope limitation\n    47→- **WHEN** the agent has determined the work scope through the startup protocol\n    48→- **THEN** the agent only executes tasks within that scope and does not actively expand the scope\n    49→\n    50→### Requirement: Session Shutdown Protocol\n    51→Session ending MUST leave code in a mergeable state. Progress tracking is handled automatically by tools.\n    52→\n    53→Shutdown steps:\n    54→1. Ensure all modified code runs correctly\n    55→2. Run `se3 handoff` — automatically commits and generates session summary in progress.md\n    56→3. Update openspec change status if applicable\n    57→\n    58→### Requirement: Session Commit Cadence\n    59→\n    60→The system SHALL define when commits occur during a session.\n    61→\n    62→Commits SHOULD occur mid-session when a distinct, working unit of change is complete — not accumulated into a single commit with unrelated changes.\n    63→\n    64→**When to commit mid-session:**\n    65→- After completing a coherent unit of work that passes tests\n    66→- Before starting a substantially different task that would muddy the commit message\n    67→- Before context clearing (/new) if there are completed changes to preserve\n    68→\n    69→**Commit sequence:**\n    70→1. Run tests — do NOT commit with failing tests\n    71→2. Stage files\n    72→3. Write message with context for next session\n    73→4. Commit (via `se3 commit` — automatically appends progress entry)\n    74→\n    75→**Commit Rules:**\n    76→- Commit when a meaningful unit of work is complete — not tied to /new or any mechanical trigger\n    77→- Do not batch unrelated changes into one commit\n    78→- Commit messages MUST include summary of changes and context for the next session\n    79→\n    80→#### Scenario: Mid-session commit\n    81→- **WHEN** a distinct unit of work is complete and tested\n    82→- **THEN** agent commits before starting the next unit\n    83→\n    84→### Requirement: Session Context Clearing\n    85→\n    86→The system SHALL define when context is cleared during a session.\n    87→\n    88→Context SHOULD be cleared when it approaches saturation or when switching to a substantially different task.\n    89→\n    90→Context SHOULD NOT be cleared mechanically after every task group — agents SHOULD continue if there is context budget and the next task benefits from current context.\n    91→\n    92→#### Scenario: Normal shutdown\n    93→- **WHEN** agent completes the current scope\n    94→- **THEN** agent runs `se3 handoff`, code is mergeable, progress is updated automatically\n    95→\n    96→#### Scenario: Context approaching saturation\n    97→- **WHEN** context window is nearly full\n    98→- **THEN** agent prioritizes shutdown protocol (at minimum: `se3 commit` current work)\n    99→\n   100→### Requirement: Progress Tracking File\n   101→The system SHALL use progress.md as the cumulative cross-session progress record. Progress entries are appended automatically by `se3 commit` and `se3 handoff` — agents do NOT manually maintain progress.md.\n   102→\n   103→progress.md contains:\n   104→- Auto-appended commit entries in \"Current Session\" section (by `se3 commit`)\n   105→- Formal session records generated by `se3 handoff`\n   106→- Collab session reports generated by orchestrator do_complete()\n   107→\n   108→#### Scenario: Auto-append commit entry\n   109→- **WHEN** `se3 commit` succeeds\n   110→- **THEN** a one-line commit record is automatically appended to progress.md Current Session section\n   111→\n   112→#### Scenario: Finalize session\n   113→- **WHEN** `se3 handoff` is executed\n   114→- **THEN** the Current Session section is replaced with a formal dated session record including commits, files changed, and next steps\n   115→\n   116→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"e9fbdf00-a716-45a9-85cd-9dd9c78fb564"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_0ZOz7J20cAVYIvM0kBUIOpQM","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# status-diagnostics Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Provide real-time project status diagnostics computed from live state (git, collab, human-calls, openspec). Replaces the manual status.md file with computed output.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Live State Computation\n     7→The system SHALL compute project status in real-time from the file system and git, not from a manually maintained status.md file.\n     8→\n     9→Sources:\n    10→- `git status` / `git log` for branch, uncommitted changes, recent activity\n    11→- `openspec/changes/` for active (non-archived) changes\n    12→- `.collab/config.json` + `.collab/tasks/*.json` for collaboration session state\n    13→- `human-calls/` for pending/responded requests\n    14→\n    15→#### Scenario: Compute git status\n    16→- **WHEN** `se3 status` is run\n    17→- **THEN** it shows current branch, uncommitted change count, and recent commits from live git state\n    18→\n    19→#### Scenario: Detect active changes\n    20→- **WHEN** `se3 status` is run and `openspec/changes/` has non-archived directories\n    21→- **THEN** it lists them as active changes\n    22→\n    23→#### Scenario: Show collab status\n    24→- **WHEN** `se3 status` is run and `.collab/config.json` exists with status \"active\"\n    25→- **THEN** it shows collab objective and task statuses\n    26→\n    27→### Requirement: Human-Calls Check\n    28→The system SHALL check `human-calls/` for pending or responded requests.\n    29→\n    30→#### Scenario: Unprocessed response\n    31→- **WHEN** a human-call file has `status: responded` but has not been processed\n    32→- **THEN** `se3 status` warns that response may need processing\n    33→\n    34→#### Scenario: Long-pending call\n    35→- **WHEN** a human-call has been pending for > timeout_days\n    36→- **THEN** `se3 status` flags it as potentially stale\n    37→\n    38→### Requirement: Collab Task Diagnostics\n    39→The system SHALL check collab task states for issues.\n    40→\n    41→#### Scenario: Failed collab tasks\n    42→- **WHEN** collab tasks have status \"failed\" or \"escalated\"\n    43→- **THEN** `se3 status` warns about them with task IDs\n    44→\n    45→#### Scenario: Blocked collab tasks\n    46→- **WHEN** collab tasks have status \"blocked\"\n    47→- **THEN** `se3 status` warns and suggests checking blocked_reason\n    48→\n    49→### Requirement: Diagnostic Output\n    50→The system SHALL provide actionable diagnostic output.\n    51→\n    52→Output format options:\n    53→- `--format=text` (default): Human-readable report\n    54→- `--format=json`: Machine-parseable for automation\n    55→\n    56→#### Scenario: Healthy project\n    57→- **WHEN** all checks pass\n    58→- **THEN** `se3 status` reports \"All diagnostics passed\"\n    59→\n    60→#### Scenario: Issues found\n    61→- **WHEN** one or more checks find issues\n    62→- **THEN** `se3 status` lists each issue with severity and suggested fix\n    63→\n    64→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"01072c81-71c1-474a-94be-fc00a1452285"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_yN83LnFlQ3gbD7TvZeG1VJVw","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# spec-lint Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Spec File Discovery\n     7→The system SHALL discover all spec files in `openspec/specs/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/`.\n     8→\n     9→#### Scenario: Discover specs in standard location\n    10→- **WHEN** `se3 lint` is run\n    11→- **THEN** it finds all `spec.md` files under `openspec/specs/*/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/*/`\n    12→\n    13→### Requirement: Required Field Validation\n    14→The system SHALL validate that each spec file contains required fields.\n    15→\n    16→Required fields:\n    17→- `# <name> Specification` - Title header\n    18→- `## Purpose` - Purpose section explaining what this spec governs\n    19→- `## Requirements` - Requirements section with at least one requirement\n    20→- Each requirement MUST have scenarios (WHEN/THEN) or be marked as `**SHOULD**`/`**MAY**`\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Spec with missing Purpose\n    23→- **WHEN** a spec file lacks a Purpose section\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports an error with file path and line number\n    25→\n    26→#### Scenario: Spec with untestable requirement\n    27→- **WHEN** a requirement uses SHALL but has no WHEN/THEN scenario\n    28→- **THEN** `se3 lint` warns that the requirement may not be verifiable\n    29→\n    30→### Requirement: Scenario Format Validation\n    31→The system SHALL validate WHEN/THEN scenario format.\n    32→\n    33→Valid format:\n    34→```\n    35→#### Scenario: <name>\n    36→- **WHEN** <condition>\n    37→- **THEN** <expected outcome>\n    38→```\n    39→\n    40→#### Scenario: Malformed scenario\n    41→- **WHEN** a scenario is missing THEN clause\n    42→- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports the error with context\n    43→\n    44→### Requirement: Exit Codes\n    45→The system SHALL return appropriate exit codes.\n    46→\n    47→Exit codes:\n    48→- `0` - All specs valid\n    49→- `1` - One or more errors found\n    50→- `2` - Configuration or runtime error\n    51→\n    52→#### Scenario: CI integration\n    53→- **WHEN** `se3 lint` runs in CI pipeline\n    54→- **THEN** exit code 1 causes pipeline failure on spec violations\n    55→\n    56→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"2fd8607b-5053-4dc7-8fa3-9ab2498d5240"}
