{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_sVZZrOk2u0cEvXywt2fhI6c0","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# SE 3.0 Best Practices\n     2→\n     3→## 1. Human Calls\n     4→\n     5→### When to use sync mode\n     6→- Human is present and can answer immediately\n     7→- Project direction (first bootstrap)\n     8→- Quick decisions, requirement clarification\n     9→\n    10→### When to use async mode\n    11→- Offline operations (deploy, create accounts)\n    12→- Human has left for the day\n    13→- Question needs research time\n    14→- Cross-session pending requests\n    15→\n    16→### Writing good human calls\n    17→- Provide full context (why is input needed?)\n    18→- For decisions: list options with trade-off analysis\n    19→- State which tasks are blocked\n    20→\n    21→### When NOT to issue a human call\n    22→- Pure implementation details\n    23→- Already specified in openspec specs\n    24→- Answerable through docs or code search\n    25→\n    26→## 2. Specs and Adaptive Formality\n    27→\n    28→### Specs as source of truth\n    29→- OpenSpec specs are the **source of truth for requirements**\n    30→- In agent team mode, specs are **contracts between agents** — write them precisely enough that an agent with no other context can implement from them\n    31→- Use `openspec list --specs` for a full requirements overview\n    32→\n    33→### Match process to scope\n    34→- **Don't over-formalize**: a bug fix doesn't need a proposal, specs, design, and tasks\n    35→- **Don't under-formalize**: a new capability that sub-agents will implement needs detailed specs with scenarios\n    36→- Rule of thumb: if you can describe the change fully in a commit message, skip the openspec change\n    37→\n    38→### When to write a design doc\n    39→- Cross-cutting changes affecting multiple modules\n    40→- Architecture decisions where sub-agents need to make consistent choices\n    41→- New external dependencies or significant data model changes\n    42→- Skip for everything else\n    43→\n    44→### Writing good specs (agent contracts)\n    45→- Each requirement must be implementable by an agent reading only the spec\n    46→- Scenarios (WHEN/THEN) serve as acceptance criteria — a reviewer agent uses them to verify\n    47→- Avoid vague language: \"should handle errors gracefully\" → \"SHALL return HTTP 400 with error detail when input validation fails\"\n    48→\n    49→## 3. Commits\n    50→\n    51→- Commit when a **meaningful unit of work** is complete\n    52→- NOT on a timer, NOT tied to /new, NOT after every task group\n    53→- A good commit represents a coherent change that makes sense on its own\n    54→- Message must include context for the next session\n    55→\n    56→```\n    57→[change-name] Completed XYZ\n    58→\n    59→Status: 3/5 tasks done\n    60→Note: edge case in module Y needs attention\n    61→Next: finish remaining tasks, focus on error handling\n    62→```\n    63→\n    64→## 4. Context Management\n    65→\n    66→- Clear context (/new) when **saturated** or when **switching to a different task domain**\n    67→- Do NOT clear mechanically — if the next task benefits from current context, continue\n    68→- Before clearing, ensure you've committed if there's meaningful work\n    69→\n    70→## 5. Session Management\n    71→\n    72→### Progressive startup\n    73→- Read `status.md` first for current session state (runtime dashboard)\n    74→- Read `progress.md` latest entry for cross-session history\n    75→- Read `git log --oneline -5`\n    76→- Don't pre-read specs — load them when the task needs them\n    77→\n    78→### status.md — the diagnostic dashboard\n    79→\n    80→**Purpose**: One file to answer \"what is happening right now?\"\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"tool_Es7mxUkJda2Fynqc4h0KnPJS","session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"6e7fb8f3-5b97-4954-a184-0ed2db0269ce"}
