{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_p5Z55P4MmgIMna1DU8ESwcjz","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# session-protocol Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the session lifecycle protocol for SE 3.0 agents. This spec governs progressive startup (se3 status → progress.md → scope determination), execution boundaries, shutdown procedures, and tool-enforced progress tracking across sessions.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Session Startup Protocol\n     7→The system SHALL define a progressive session startup protocol. The agent MUST locate current state with minimal context, then load more on demand.\n     8→\n     9→Startup steps:\n    10→1. Verify `openspec` CLI is available; if not, ask the human to install it. If available but `openspec/` directory does not exist, run `openspec init`.\n    11→2. Run `se3 status` for computed project state (git, collab, human-calls)\n    12→3. Read `head -50 progress.md` + `git log --oneline -5`\n    13→4. Scan `human-calls/` for responded but unprocessed requests\n    14→5. Check `openspec/changes/` for active changes and `openspec/specs/` for current capabilities\n    15→6. Determine session scope based on progress \"next steps\" + active changes\n    16→7. Load additional files only when the task requires them\n    17→\n    18→If step 3 finds no progress.md and no git history → **first-time bootstrap**:\n    19→- Ask the human (sync human call): \"What should this project do?\"\n    20→- Create an openspec change from the response (proposal captures the intent)\n    21→- Create `progress.md`\n    22→\n    23→#### Scenario: OpenSpec not installed\n    24→- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` command is not found\n    25→- **THEN** agent asks the human to install it via sync human call and does not proceed with spec-related work until resolved\n    26→\n    27→#### Scenario: OpenSpec not initialized\n    28→- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` is available but `openspec/` directory does not exist\n    29→- **THEN** agent runs `openspec init` before proceeding\n    30→\n    31→#### Scenario: Mature project startup\n    32→- **WHEN** agent starts a session with existing progress.md and git history\n    33→- **THEN** agent runs `se3 status`, reads progress.md and git log, then determines scope without reading all spec files upfront\n    34→\n    35→#### Scenario: First-time bootstrap\n    36→- **WHEN** agent starts a session with no progress.md and no git history\n    37→- **THEN** agent asks the human what to build via human call, creates an openspec change from the response\n    38→\n    39→#### Scenario: On-demand deep loading\n    40→- **WHEN** agent needs details about a specific capability during execution\n    41→- **THEN** agent reads the relevant spec file at that point, not during startup\n    42→\n    43→### Requirement: Session Execution Boundary\n    44→Each session MUST focus on a limited scope of work and MUST NOT attempt to complete too many tasks in a single session.\n    45→\n    46→#### Scenario: Session scope limitation\n    47→- **WHEN** the agent has determined the work scope through the startup protocol\n    48→- **THEN** the agent only executes tasks within that scope and does not actively expand the scope\n    49→\n    50→### Requirement: Session Shutdown Protocol\n    51→Session ending MUST leave code in a mergeable state. Progress tracking is handled automatically by tools.\n    52→\n    53→Shutdown steps:\n    54→1. Ensure all modified code runs correctly\n    55→2. Run `se3 handoff` — automatically commits and generates session summary in progress.md\n    56→3. Update openspec change status if applicable\n    57→\n    58→### Requirement: Session Commit Cadence\n    59→\n    60→The system SHALL define when commits occur during a session.\n    61→\n    62→Commits SHOULD occur mid-session when a distinct, working unit of change is complete — not accumulated into a single commit with unrelated changes.\n    63→\n    64→**When to commit mid-session:**\n    65→- After completing a coherent unit of work that passes tests\n    66→- Before starting a substantially different task that would muddy the commit message\n    67→- Before context clearing (/new) if there are completed changes to preserve\n    68→\n    69→**Commit sequence:**\n    70→1. Run tests — do NOT commit with failing tests\n    71→2. Stage files\n    72→3. Write message with context for next session\n    73→4. Commit (via `se3 commit` — automatically appends progress entry)\n    74→\n    75→**Commit Rules:**\n    76→- Commit when a meaningful unit of work is complete — not tied to /new or any mechanical trigger\n    77→- Do not batch unrelated changes into one commit\n    78→- Commit messages MUST include summary of changes and context for the next session\n    79→\n    80→#### Scenario: Mid-session commit\n    81→- **WHEN** a distinct unit of work is complete and tested\n    82→- **THEN** agent commits before starting the next unit\n    83→\n    84→### Requirement: Session Context Clearing\n    85→\n    86→The system SHALL define when context is cleared during a session.\n    87→\n    88→Context SHOULD be cleared when it approaches saturation or when switching to a substantially different task.\n    89→\n    90→Context SHOULD NOT be cleared mechanically after every task group — agents SHOULD continue if there is context budget and the next task benefits from current context.\n    91→\n    92→#### Scenario: Normal shutdown\n    93→- **WHEN** agent completes the current scope\n    94→- **THEN** agent runs `se3 handoff`, code is mergeable, progress is updated automatically\n    95→\n    96→#### Scenario: Context approaching saturation\n    97→- **WHEN** context window is nearly full\n    98→- **THEN** agent prioritizes shutdown protocol (at minimum: `se3 commit` current work)\n    99→\n   100→### Requirement: Progress Tracking File\n   101→The system SHALL use progress.md as the cumulative cross-session progress record. Progress entries are appended automatically by `se3 commit` and `se3 handoff` — agents do NOT manually maintain progress.md.\n   102→\n   103→progress.md contains:\n   104→- Auto-appended commit entries in \"Current Session\" section (by `se3 commit`)\n   105→- Formal session records generated by `se3 handoff`\n   106→- Collab session reports generated by orchestrator do_complete()\n   107→\n   108→#### Scenario: Auto-append commit entry\n   109→- **WHEN** `se3 commit` succeeds\n   110→- **THEN** a one-line commit record is automatically appended to progress.md Current Session section\n   111→\n   112→#### Scenario: Finalize session\n   113→- **WHEN** `se3 handoff` is executed\n   114→- **THEN** the Current Session section is replaced with a formal dated session record including commits, files changed, and next steps\n   115→\n   116→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"99dd4abb-75e5-426e-849f-d440c0f52a42","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/openspec/specs/session-protocol/spec.md","content":"# session-protocol Specification\n\n## Purpose\nDefine the session lifecycle protocol for SE 3.0 agents. This spec governs progressive startup (se3 status → progress.md → scope determination), execution boundaries, shutdown procedures, and tool-enforced progress tracking across sessions.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Session Startup Protocol\nThe system SHALL define a progressive session startup protocol. The agent MUST locate current state with minimal context, then load more on demand.\n\nStartup steps:\n1. Verify `openspec` CLI is available; if not, ask the human to install it. If available but `openspec/` directory does not exist, run `openspec init`.\n2. Run `se3 status` for computed project state (git, collab, human-calls)\n3. Read `head -50 progress.md` + `git log --oneline -5`\n4. Scan `human-calls/` for responded but unprocessed requests\n5. Check `openspec/changes/` for active changes and `openspec/specs/` for current capabilities\n6. Determine session scope based on progress \"next steps\" + active changes\n7. Load additional files only when the task requires them\n\nIf step 3 finds no progress.md and no git history → **first-time bootstrap**:\n- Ask the human (sync human call): \"What should this project do?\"\n- Create an openspec change from the response (proposal captures the intent)\n- Create `progress.md`\n\n#### Scenario: OpenSpec not installed\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` command is not found\n- **THEN** agent asks the human to install it via sync human call and does not proceed with spec-related work until resolved\n\n#### Scenario: OpenSpec not initialized\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` is available but `openspec/` directory does not exist\n- **THEN** agent runs `openspec init` before proceeding\n\n#### Scenario: Mature project startup\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session with existing progress.md and git history\n- **THEN** agent runs `se3 status`, reads progress.md and git log, then determines scope without reading all spec files upfront\n\n#### Scenario: First-time bootstrap\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session with no progress.md and no git history\n- **THEN** agent asks the human what to build via human call, creates an openspec change from the response\n\n#### Scenario: On-demand deep loading\n- **WHEN** agent needs details about a specific capability during execution\n- **THEN** agent reads the relevant spec file at that point, not during startup\n\n### Requirement: Session Execution Boundary\nEach session MUST focus on a limited scope of work and MUST NOT attempt to complete too many tasks in a single session.\n\n#### Scenario: Session scope limitation\n- **WHEN** the agent has determined the work scope through the startup protocol\n- **THEN** the agent only executes tasks within that scope and does not actively expand the scope\n\n### Requirement: Session Shutdown Protocol\nSession ending MUST leave code in a mergeable state. Progress tracking is handled automatically by tools.\n\nShutdown steps:\n1. Ensure all modified code runs correctly\n2. Run `se3 handoff` — automatically commits and generates session summary in progress.md\n3. Update openspec change status if applicable\n\n### Requirement: Session Commit Cadence\n\nThe system SHALL define when commits occur during a session.\n\nCommits SHOULD occur mid-session when a distinct, working unit of change is complete — not accumulated into a single commit with unrelated changes.\n\n**When to commit mid-session:**\n- After completing a coherent unit of work that passes tests\n- Before starting a substantially different task that would muddy the commit message\n- Before context clearing (/new) if there are completed changes to preserve\n\n**Commit sequence:**\n1. Run tests — do NOT commit with failing tests\n2. Stage files\n3. Write message with context for next session\n4. Commit (via `se3 commit` — automatically appends progress entry)\n\n**Commit Rules:**\n- Commit when a meaningful unit of work is complete — not tied to /new or any mechanical trigger\n- Do not batch unrelated changes into one commit\n- Commit messages MUST include summary of changes and context for the next session\n\n#### Scenario: Mid-session commit\n- **WHEN** a distinct unit of work is complete and tested\n- **THEN** agent commits before starting the next unit\n\n### Requirement: Session Context Clearing\n\nThe system SHALL define when context is cleared during a session.\n\nContext SHOULD be cleared when it approaches saturation or when switching to a substantially different task.\n\nContext SHOULD NOT be cleared mechanically after every task group — agents SHOULD continue if there is context budget and the next task benefits from current context.\n\n#### Scenario: Normal shutdown\n- **WHEN** agent completes the current scope\n- **THEN** agent runs `se3 handoff`, code is mergeable, progress is updated automatically\n\n#### Scenario: Context approaching saturation\n- **WHEN** context window is nearly full\n- **THEN** agent prioritizes shutdown protocol (at minimum: `se3 commit` current work)\n\n### Requirement: Progress Tracking File\nThe system SHALL use progress.md as the cumulative cross-session progress record. Progress entries are appended automatically by `se3 commit` and `se3 handoff` — agents do NOT manually maintain progress.md.\n\nprogress.md contains:\n- Auto-appended commit entries in \"Current Session\" section (by `se3 commit`)\n- Formal session records generated by `se3 handoff`\n- Collab session reports generated by orchestrator do_complete()\n\n#### Scenario: Auto-append commit entry\n- **WHEN** `se3 commit` succeeds\n- **THEN** a one-line commit record is automatically appended to progress.md Current Session section\n\n#### Scenario: Finalize session\n- **WHEN** `se3 handoff` is executed\n- **THEN** the Current Session section is replaced with a formal dated session record including commits, files changed, and next steps\n\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_M9HjcQu05uV4iaoK9fVrSHIv","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# spec-lint Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Spec File Discovery\n     7→The system SHALL discover all spec files in `openspec/specs/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/`.\n     8→\n     9→#### Scenario: Discover specs in standard location\n    10→- **WHEN** `se3 lint` is run\n    11→- **THEN** it finds all `spec.md` files under `openspec/specs/*/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/*/`\n    12→\n    13→### Requirement: Required Field Validation\n    14→The system SHALL validate that each spec file contains required fields.\n    15→\n    16→Required fields:\n    17→- `# <name> Specification` - Title header\n    18→- `## Purpose` - Purpose section explaining what this spec governs\n    19→- `## Requirements` - Requirements section with at least one requirement\n    20→- Each requirement MUST have scenarios (WHEN/THEN) or be marked as `**SHOULD**`/`**MAY**`\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Spec with missing Purpose\n    23→- **WHEN** a spec file lacks a Purpose section\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports an error with file path and line number\n    25→\n    26→#### Scenario: Spec with untestable requirement\n    27→- **WHEN** a requirement uses SHALL but has no WHEN/THEN scenario\n    28→- **THEN** `se3 lint` warns that the requirement may not be verifiable\n    29→\n    30→### Requirement: Scenario Format Validation\n    31→The system SHALL validate WHEN/THEN scenario format.\n    32→\n    33→Valid format:\n    34→```\n    35→#### Scenario: <name>\n    36→- **WHEN** <condition>\n    37→- **THEN** <expected outcome>\n    38→```\n    39→\n    40→#### Scenario: Malformed scenario\n    41→- **WHEN** a scenario is missing THEN clause\n    42→- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports the error with context\n    43→\n    44→### Requirement: Exit Codes\n    45→The system SHALL return appropriate exit codes.\n    46→\n    47→Exit codes:\n    48→- `0` - All specs valid\n    49→- `1` - One or more errors found\n    50→- `2` - Configuration or runtime error\n    51→\n    52→#### Scenario: CI integration\n    53→- **WHEN** `se3 lint` runs in CI pipeline\n    54→- **THEN** exit code 1 causes pipeline failure on spec violations\n    55→\n    56→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"37633d32-3df9-4f05-b6d0-81c28341b45d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/openspec/specs/spec-lint/spec.md","content":"# spec-lint Specification\n\n## Purpose\nTBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Spec File Discovery\nThe system SHALL discover all spec files in `openspec/specs/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/`.\n\n#### Scenario: Discover specs in standard location\n- **WHEN** `se3 lint` is run\n- **THEN** it finds all `spec.md` files under `openspec/specs/*/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/*/`\n\n### Requirement: Required Field Validation\nThe system SHALL validate that each spec file contains required fields.\n\nRequired fields:\n- `# <name> Specification` - Title header\n- `## Purpose` - Purpose section explaining what this spec governs\n- `## Requirements` - Requirements section with at least one requirement\n- Each requirement MUST have scenarios (WHEN/THEN) or be marked as `**SHOULD**`/`**MAY**`\n\n#### Scenario: Spec with missing Purpose\n- **WHEN** a spec file lacks a Purpose section\n- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports an error with file path and line number\n\n#### Scenario: Spec with untestable requirement\n- **WHEN** a requirement uses SHALL but has no WHEN/THEN scenario\n- **THEN** `se3 lint` warns that the requirement may not be verifiable\n\n### Requirement: Scenario Format Validation\nThe system SHALL validate WHEN/THEN scenario format.\n\nValid format:\n```\n#### Scenario: <name>\n- **WHEN** <condition>\n- **THEN** <expected outcome>\n```\n\n#### Scenario: Malformed scenario\n- **WHEN** a scenario is missing THEN clause\n- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports the error with context\n\n### Requirement: Exit Codes\nThe system SHALL return appropriate exit codes.\n\nExit codes:\n- `0` - All specs valid\n- `1` - One or more errors found\n- `2` - Configuration or runtime error\n\n#### Scenario: CI integration\n- **WHEN** `se3 lint` runs in CI pipeline\n- **THEN** exit code 1 causes pipeline failure on spec violations\n\n","numLines":56,"startLine":1,"totalLines":56}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_mrwlNKv1Vgcd02RhiUj9xZej","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# requirement-intake Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change requirement-capture. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Three-Source Requirement Intake\n     7→The system SHALL accept new requirements from three distinct sources and route them through a unified `openspec change` creation process.\n     8→\n     9→**Source 1: Autonomous Discovery (Agent-Initiated)**\n    10→- Trigger: Agent identifies missing capability during implementation\n    11→- Flow: Agent creates new openspec change directly → proposal captures the discovered requirement\n    12→- Marker: `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` in proposal\n    13→\n    14→**Source 2: Human-MCP Call (Requested)**\n    15→- Trigger: Agent issues human call asking for requirements input\n    16→- Flow: Human responds → Agent parses response → Creates openspec change\n    17→- Marker: `[Source: human-mcp]` in proposal\n    18→\n    19→**Source 3: Human-Initiated (External)**\n    20→- Trigger: Human provides new requirement through any interaction (at natural conversation boundary or during active session)\n    21→- Flow: Agent recognizes human-initiated input → Creates new openspec change\n    22→- Marker: `[Source: human-initiated]` in proposal\n    23→- Note: \"Human-initiated\" is about **who originated the requirement** (human, not agent), not **how it was delivered** (interrupt or natural turn). All human-proposed requirements go through this path.\n    24→\n    25→#### Scenario: Autonomous discovery during implementation\n    26→- **WHEN** an agent realizes a needed feature wasn't in the original spec during coding\n    27→- **THEN** the agent creates a new openspec change with `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` marker\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Human provides requirements via MCP response\n    30→- **WHEN** a human fills in the Response section of a requirements-request call\n    31→- **THEN** the agent parses it and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-mcp]` marker\n    32→\n    33→#### Scenario: Human provides requirement through any interaction\n    34→- **WHEN** a human provides a new requirement (whether at natural conversation boundary or by interrupting active session)\n    35→- **THEN** the agent recognizes this as human-initiated input and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-initiated]` marker\n    36→- **NOTE** The distinction between \"interrupt\" and \"natural turn\" is not functionally significant; both are human-initiated requirements\n    37→\n    38→### Requirement: Unified Change Creation\n    39→All three intake sources SHALL result in the same downstream process: an openspec change with proposal → specs → implementation.\n    40→\n    41→#### Scenario: Consistent processing regardless of source\n    42→- **WHEN** any of the three sources triggers\n    43→- **THEN** the resulting openspec change follows the same SDD workflow (proposal → specs → design → tasks → code → verify → archive)\n    44→\n    45→### Requirement: Interruption Context Preservation\n    46→When Source 3 (human interrupt) occurs, the system SHALL preserve context of interrupted work.\n    47→\n    48→**Context preservation options:**\n    49→1. **Stack-based**: Push current change to stack, create new interrupt change, pop on completion\n    50→2. **Link-based**: New change references \"interrupted from: <change-id>\"\n    51→3. **Checkpoint-based**: Mark checkpoint in current change, resume from there after interrupt\n    52→\n    53→#### Scenario: Resume after interrupt\n    54→- **WHEN** a human-interrupt change completes\n    55→- **THEN** agent asks: \"Resume previous work on `<change-id>`? [Y/n]\"\n    56→\n    57→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"1255b0c2-71df-43d9-87a6-ac1e84446cee","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/openspec/specs/requirement-intake/spec.md","content":"# requirement-intake Specification\n\n## Purpose\nTBD - created by archiving change requirement-capture. Update Purpose after archive.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Three-Source Requirement Intake\nThe system SHALL accept new requirements from three distinct sources and route them through a unified `openspec change` creation process.\n\n**Source 1: Autonomous Discovery (Agent-Initiated)**\n- Trigger: Agent identifies missing capability during implementation\n- Flow: Agent creates new openspec change directly → proposal captures the discovered requirement\n- Marker: `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` in proposal\n\n**Source 2: Human-MCP Call (Requested)**\n- Trigger: Agent issues human call asking for requirements input\n- Flow: Human responds → Agent parses response → Creates openspec change\n- Marker: `[Source: human-mcp]` in proposal\n\n**Source 3: Human-Initiated (External)**\n- Trigger: Human provides new requirement through any interaction (at natural conversation boundary or during active session)\n- Flow: Agent recognizes human-initiated input → Creates new openspec change\n- Marker: `[Source: human-initiated]` in proposal\n- Note: \"Human-initiated\" is about **who originated the requirement** (human, not agent), not **how it was delivered** (interrupt or natural turn). All human-proposed requirements go through this path.\n\n#### Scenario: Autonomous discovery during implementation\n- **WHEN** an agent realizes a needed feature wasn't in the original spec during coding\n- **THEN** the agent creates a new openspec change with `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` marker\n\n#### Scenario: Human provides requirements via MCP response\n- **WHEN** a human fills in the Response section of a requirements-request call\n- **THEN** the agent parses it and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-mcp]` marker\n\n#### Scenario: Human provides requirement through any interaction\n- **WHEN** a human provides a new requirement (whether at natural conversation boundary or by interrupting active session)\n- **THEN** the agent recognizes this as human-initiated input and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-initiated]` marker\n- **NOTE** The distinction between \"interrupt\" and \"natural turn\" is not functionally significant; both are human-initiated requirements\n\n### Requirement: Unified Change Creation\nAll three intake sources SHALL result in the same downstream process: an openspec change with proposal → specs → implementation.\n\n#### Scenario: Consistent processing regardless of source\n- **WHEN** any of the three sources triggers\n- **THEN** the resulting openspec change follows the same SDD workflow (proposal → specs → design → tasks → code → verify → archive)\n\n### Requirement: Interruption Context Preservation\nWhen Source 3 (human interrupt) occurs, the system SHALL preserve context of interrupted work.\n\n**Context preservation options:**\n1. **Stack-based**: Push current change to stack, create new interrupt change, pop on completion\n2. **Link-based**: New change references \"interrupted from: <change-id>\"\n3. **Checkpoint-based**: Mark checkpoint in current change, resume from there after interrupt\n\n#### Scenario: Resume after interrupt\n- **WHEN** a human-interrupt change completes\n- **THEN** agent asks: \"Resume previous work on `<change-id>`? [Y/n]\"\n\n","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_HlJznhPrPKoRg7fs0jtbgefe","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# change-verifier Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Spec Scenario Extraction\n     7→The system SHALL extract all WHEN/THEN scenarios from a change's specs.\n     8→\n     9→#### Scenario: Extract scenarios from change\n    10→- **WHEN** `se3 verify --change <name>` is run\n    11→- **THEN** it parses all spec files in `openspec/changes/<name>/specs/`\n    12→- **AND** extracts a list of scenario IDs (format: `<spec-name>/<scenario-name>`)\n    13→\n    14→### Requirement: Implementation Detection\n    15→The system SHALL detect evidence of scenario implementation.\n    16→\n    17→Detection methods (in order of confidence):\n    18→1. Test file with `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"<id>\")` decorator\n    19→2. Code comment `# Verify: <scenario-id>`\n    20→3. Spec archive with \"implemented\" marker\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Find test marker\n    23→- **WHEN** a test file contains `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"session-protocol/first-time-bootstrap\")`\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 verify` marks that scenario as covered\n    25→\n    26→### Requirement: Coverage Report\n    27→The system SHALL generate a coverage report showing implemented vs missing scenarios.\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Incomplete implementation\n    30→- **WHEN** some scenarios have no detected implementation\n    31→- **THEN** `se3 verify` lists uncovered scenarios with file paths\n    32→- **AND** exits with code 1\n    33→\n    34→#### Scenario: Complete implementation\n    35→- **WHEN** all scenarios have detected implementation\n    36→- **THEN** `se3 verify` reports success\n    37→- **AND** exits with code 0\n    38→\n    39→### Requirement: Skip Annotation\n    40→The system SHALL support explicitly skipping scenarios.\n    41→\n    42→#### Scenario: Skip with reason\n    43→- **WHEN** a spec contains `<!-- verify-skip: reason -->` before a scenario\n    44→- **THEN** that scenario is excluded from coverage requirements\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"f41744b2-89b7-41ea-a819-5608fb3ef3ae","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/openspec/specs/change-verifier/spec.md","content":"# change-verifier Specification\n\n## Purpose\nTBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Spec Scenario Extraction\nThe system SHALL extract all WHEN/THEN scenarios from a change's specs.\n\n#### Scenario: Extract scenarios from change\n- **WHEN** `se3 verify --change <name>` is run\n- **THEN** it parses all spec files in `openspec/changes/<name>/specs/`\n- **AND** extracts a list of scenario IDs (format: `<spec-name>/<scenario-name>`)\n\n### Requirement: Implementation Detection\nThe system SHALL detect evidence of scenario implementation.\n\nDetection methods (in order of confidence):\n1. Test file with `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"<id>\")` decorator\n2. Code comment `# Verify: <scenario-id>`\n3. Spec archive with \"implemented\" marker\n\n#### Scenario: Find test marker\n- **WHEN** a test file contains `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"session-protocol/first-time-bootstrap\")`\n- **THEN** `se3 verify` marks that scenario as covered\n\n### Requirement: Coverage Report\nThe system SHALL generate a coverage report showing implemented vs missing scenarios.\n\n#### Scenario: Incomplete implementation\n- **WHEN** some scenarios have no detected implementation\n- **THEN** `se3 verify` lists uncovered scenarios with file paths\n- **AND** exits with code 1\n\n#### Scenario: Complete implementation\n- **WHEN** all scenarios have detected implementation\n- **THEN** `se3 verify` reports success\n- **AND** exits with code 0\n\n### Requirement: Skip Annotation\nThe system SHALL support explicitly skipping scenarios.\n\n#### Scenario: Skip with reason\n- **WHEN** a spec contains `<!-- verify-skip: reason -->` before a scenario\n- **THEN** that scenario is excluded from coverage requirements\n\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_xmAsEZfyA7hrPYwEcmhrLAxb","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Active\n     2→- 有未解决的人类call可以在se3 collab --status时提示\n     3→- 要有个一键执行start-work-done整个流程的command，包括se3 cli和claude的command\n     4→- progress.md的修改似乎在提交之前，是否要将它加入到提交流程中\n     5→- 你确定openspec的init加到了流程中吗？为什么这么久了这个项目还是没有init后的openspec commands？\n     6→- se3的cli version还有存在的价值吗？2.x版本后，应该会有两个版本，一个是commands的版本，一个是se3程序的版本。这两个版本应该统一为一个版本还是需要两个版本来管理呢？\n     7→- human calls的归档\n     8→- human从交互界面之外的输入方式，以及输入的归档。\n     9→- SE3.md没有存在的意义，文档可以加到se3的帮助里，但是需要有地方记录已安装的commands的版本，有什么更好的方式吗？但是话又说回来，比如说openspec文件夹作为项目的唯一真相来源，这一点，是否是应该让agent全局知晓而非要触发/se3的command才生效？那这样看来SE3.md是否还是应该存在？\n    10→# Old\n    11→- 版本问题，有一次更改规则之后，并没有更新版本，这个项目有没有一个更新版本的流程？更改了规则的一行不应该更改版本吗？\n    12→- scripts里面的内容是这个项目相关的还是在se3发布的包里会包含的还是SE3规则下的项目会生成的？\n    13→- human calls的检测规则是，只要call的下面的人类回应里有内容，就算是完成了answer收集是吗？这个是怎么收集的？定时轮询吗？\n    14→- human calls设定语言，用设定的语言进行human call\n    15→- 健康度检查的timeout是否太粗糙？有些任务就是很长怎么办？是不是应该按照它上次有token产生或读取来计算timeout？\n    16→- 是否应当从一个se3的入口来进入开发而非使用claude来进入开发？如果是，这个入口应当可以设置claude的入口命令。另外，如果从另外的入口进入开发的话，是否要保留从claude进入开发的能力？\n    17→- 是否应当增加人类在MCP call和运行claude之外的输入的方式？比如说某个prompt文件？\n    18→- 不同的claude入口命令，在hang之后切换\n    19→- versioning部分不应该在SE3.md里，它是这个项目要考虑的事情，而不是SE3标准里应该包含的内容。\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa649a33-92f6-4c3e-90ea-c763bdcdac3a","uuid":"ae266eb4-ee54-4614-9072-23bcfaf9cd86","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/issues.md","content":"# Active\n- 有未解决的人类call可以在se3 collab --status时提示\n- 要有个一键执行start-work-done整个流程的command，包括se3 cli和claude的command\n- progress.md的修改似乎在提交之前，是否要将它加入到提交流程中\n- 你确定openspec的init加到了流程中吗？为什么这么久了这个项目还是没有init后的openspec commands？\n- se3的cli version还有存在的价值吗？2.x版本后，应该会有两个版本，一个是commands的版本，一个是se3程序的版本。这两个版本应该统一为一个版本还是需要两个版本来管理呢？\n- human calls的归档\n- human从交互界面之外的输入方式，以及输入的归档。\n- SE3.md没有存在的意义，文档可以加到se3的帮助里，但是需要有地方记录已安装的commands的版本，有什么更好的方式吗？但是话又说回来，比如说openspec文件夹作为项目的唯一真相来源，这一点，是否是应该让agent全局知晓而非要触发/se3的command才生效？那这样看来SE3.md是否还是应该存在？\n# Old\n- 版本问题，有一次更改规则之后，并没有更新版本，这个项目有没有一个更新版本的流程？更改了规则的一行不应该更改版本吗？\n- scripts里面的内容是这个项目相关的还是在se3发布的包里会包含的还是SE3规则下的项目会生成的？\n- human calls的检测规则是，只要call的下面的人类回应里有内容，就算是完成了answer收集是吗？这个是怎么收集的？定时轮询吗？\n- human calls设定语言，用设定的语言进行human call\n- 健康度检查的timeout是否太粗糙？有些任务就是很长怎么办？是不是应该按照它上次有token产生或读取来计算timeout？\n- 是否应当从一个se3的入口来进入开发而非使用claude来进入开发？如果是，这个入口应当可以设置claude的入口命令。另外，如果从另外的入口进入开发的话，是否要保留从claude进入开发的能力？\n- 是否应当增加人类在MCP call和运行claude之外的输入的方式？比如说某个prompt文件？\n- 不同的claude入口命令，在hang之后切换\n- versioning部分不应该在SE3.md里，它是这个项目要考虑的事情，而不是SE3标准里应该包含的内容。","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}}}
